{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966799,"nonce":"5gq2kkb/x3sD20b1JqVlcsborLg=","title":"Portal-UI animations now register a no-op tick_callback with the animation scheduler so the","message":"The animation scheduler's `has_active` predicate counts springs / keyframes / timelines / **tick_callbacks**. When `has_active = false` AND `wants_continuous = true` (text input focused → cursor blink), the bg thread ticks at `target_fps / 2` — half the configured cap — to save CPU on cursor-only frames.\n\nBefore this fix, portal-UI's `ui.request_animation()` only set its own per-frame `animating` flag. It didn't tell the scheduler. So portal-driven per-frame animations (sparkline tickers, ease-in widgets, anything calling `request_animation`) were invisible to `has_active` → scheduler downscaled → animations ran at half rate as soon as any text input was focused. Visible as \"FPS doesn't recover\" in node_editor_demo with a focused cn::input popover.\n\n`keyframe_canvas` worked correctly because its closures use `ctx.use_animated_timeline()` which puts a Timeline in `inner.timelines` — those ARE in `has_active`, so the downscale never fired. Animations that DON'T register a scheduler-visible primitive (a portal's request_animation, a canvas-kit canvas reading time directly) need to bridge themselves.\n\n**The fix lives in `Portal::frame`** (`packages/blinc_portal_ui/src/ui.rs`):\n- The portal owns an `anim_tick_handle: Option\u003cTickCallbackId\u003e`.\n- After each frame, if `any_animating == true \u0026\u0026 handle.is_none()` → `scheduler.register_tick_callback(|_dt| {})` and stash the id. The callback body is empty — the portal repaints itself via its own dirty path. Its **presence** is what matters: it makes `!tick_callbacks.is_empty()` true → `has_active = true` → no downscale.\n- If `any_animating == false \u0026\u0026 handle.is_some()` → `scheduler.remove_tick_callback(id)` and clear the slot. So idle portals don't keep a stale callback in the scheduler.\n- `Drop` removes the callback so a portal dropped mid-animation doesn't leak.\n\n`blinc_animation` is now a direct dep of `blinc_portal_ui` (added to both the git-rev'd deps block + the local-dev `[patch]` block).\n\n**Pattern for other animation drivers.** Any subsystem that paints per frame via direct time reads (canvas-kit's running-edge pulse, node-editor's hover-pulse on ports) and relies on `request_redraw` / `wants_continuous` to drive frames should follow the same shape: register a no-op tick_callback while it has live animation, remove on settle. The scheduler is the source of truth for \"is anything actually animating\"; subsystems that don't tell it suffer the half-rate downscale on focused-input pages.\n\n**Why not just remove the downscale?** It's an intentional CPU-saving optimization for the genuine \"focused text input on an otherwise idle page\" case (which IS common — long-form text editors, search boxes, login forms). Removing it would burn ~50% extra background-thread wakes on every focused-input scenario forever. The right fix is to make `has_active` accurate, not to defang it.\n\n**The runner's `cursor_only` path** in `windowed.rs` is a complementary saving at the runner level: when the only redraw signal is the cursor (no scroll, no css, no motion, no animation), the runner schedules the next wake at 400 ms (the actual blink interval). That keeps cursor-only frames cheap regardless of what the scheduler decides about cadence.","files":null}]}